WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.680
- Good morning.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.240
I'm Jim Levinsohn,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.110
Dean of the Yale Jackson
School of Global Affairs.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.670
I'd like to welcome everybody,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.643
and of course, a special
welcome to the president.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.760
President Arce's lecture

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.300
is our annual Sunrise Foundation Lecture,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.460
funded by the Sunrise Foundation.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.160
The lecture features prominent speakers

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.750
who come to speak on themes

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.320
related to political
and or economic issues

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.210
in emerging markets.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.050
And the Sunrise Foundation
also provides fellowships

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.930
to deserving graduate
students here at Yale,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.383
from emerging markets enrolled
at the Jackson School.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.430
The title of President Arce's talk is,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.840
"Emerging Economies in Latin America,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.243
the Bolivian Economic Model."

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:03.900
President Arce has been
president since November of 2020.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.870
He has served in the
Bolivian public service

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.330
for the entirety of his career,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.630
beginning with the
Central Bank of Bolivia.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.480
He has served as minister
of economic affairs

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.480
and public finance under Evo
Morales for almost 14 years.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.920
As minister, he successfully
managed the Bolivian economy

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.600
in a period of unprecedented
growth and poverty reduction.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.600
From 2006 to 2011, GDP grew by
an average of 4.6% per year,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.390
peaking at 6.8% in 2013,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.540
and, quite impressively, at the same time,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.930
unemployment decreased, inflation fell,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.290
and poverty was dramatically reduced.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.840
A pretty incredible record.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.200
He was elected president
in October of 2020,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.930
with the majority of the votes.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.730
I will note with special pride,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:07.080
and I speak here for both,
Professor Caliendo and myself,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.830
President Arce has also
taught economics and finance

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.693
at various universities.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.410
Today's talk will be moderated

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.590
by Professor Lorenzo Caliendo.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.840
Lorenzo is the Won Park Hahn

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.090
Professor of Global Affairs,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.993
and also holds professorship
at the School of Management.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.060
Lorenzo serves as Jackson's deputy dean,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.910
and is himself from Uruguay.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.140
So, Lorenzo will start
us off with some talk,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.750
some questions, and then we'll open it up.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.170
I also wanna extend a welcome
to those who are joining us,

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.390
as this is being live streamed

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.253
to many, many around the world.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.700
President Arce, thank
you so very, very much

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.130
for joining us here at Yale.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:57.446
Lorenzo.

00:02:57.446 --> 00:03:00.196
(all applauding)

00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:07.500
- After that introduction, I think it's...

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.050
President, why don't you
actually give your talk.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.210
You were thinking about
actually talking in the podium,

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.650
or you wanna do it sitting from here?

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:14.610
- I think there is--

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.603
- That's great. So, Why don't
you start, please? Thank you.

00:03:17.603 --> 00:03:18.603
- Thank you.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.360
Well, good morning to everybody.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.660
I'm very happy to be here.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.250
Professor James, to be in university,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.820
I will always have
followed all the writings

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.240
that he has done, and all
the research that he has.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.683
So, for me, it's a pleasure
to be here in this university.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:46.670
I would like to start,
just, because, I think,

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.683
the time is the worst
that we have in the world.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.260
So, I would like to
talk very briefly about

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.800
what our model is?

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.380
The principles, the basics.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.180
And also, it is very
important for us, the results.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.180
In the both fields,
economic and socio results.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.020
Which is very important, because,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:17.297
at the moment we create this model in 2005

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.180
with Professor Carlos Villegas,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.180
who was working with me at
the university at the time.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.990
We have to fight with
the neo-liberalism model,

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:37.350
who was in place in Bolivia for 20 years,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.970
since 1985 to 2005,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.570
20 years of neo-liberalism in Bolivia.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.060
And there were no model...

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.560
There were no alternative models to face,

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.000
or to replace the neo-liberalism model.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.060
So, when we started to do that,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.060
it took many years to develop this.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.820
We have to face many problems,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:13.820
and you will see a graph,
which we will compare

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.430
the neo-liberalism model

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.980
against our model,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.980
where the most important difference is

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.680
and results of each one.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.200
So, I will start, very briefly,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.790
talking about the basics of our model,

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.740
which we call Economic Social

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.453
Communitarian Productive Model.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.360
And we have to put it those words,

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.530
because it's a very long name for a model,

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.070
but it is important to say that at a time,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.110
we have to emphasize

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:56.730
that the economic policy should be,

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.513
and is always a social issue.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:06.543
Economics is mainly social science,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.020
because we have to solve
the problems of people,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.033
and the neo-liberalism
model, the market models,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.800
don't take into account, many
times, the social issues.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.690
So, that's why we call it

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:25.470
Economic Social Communitarian
Productive Model.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.920
And also, communitarian,
because we recover from the

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.230
indigenous people, the principles,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:34.500
as solidarity and other
principles and values

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.390
that the indigenous peoples have,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:41.223
and survive to the
neo-liberalism in my country.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.120
And productive, because we are changing

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.850
the Bolivian people mind,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.630
because we would like to
become a productive country,

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.380
rather than only trade, or only services.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.290
We would like to have
a productive country,

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.080
which is another way to do it.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.080
So, that is the basics of
what we put that long name.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:12.250
And the basics is that

00:07:13.902 --> 00:07:16.990
a country should grow based on

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.683
the natural resources a country has,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.493
getting economic exigence from them,

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:31.800
and which is very important,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.590
because we are the antithesis

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.510
of the Natural Resource Curse Theory.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.573
You remember in your lectures.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.310
And we demonstrate to everybody that

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.730
a country who has natural resource

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:52.730
can develop based on
the natural resources,

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.110
which is very important for us.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.710
That's why at the
beginning of our government

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.780
with President Evo Morales,

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.460
we put in place the
nationalization of hydrocarbons

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.460
and other resources, that now
under the new constitutions

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.710
is under control of the state.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.440
That's the main issue there.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:21.440
Secondly, we generate economic surpluses,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.133
exigence, we call it.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.826
In order that the state appropriates of

00:08:28.826 --> 00:08:31.323
this exigence, this surplus,

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.920
and why the state has to be here,

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.590
and that's the big issue at that time.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.170
I think, still in many countries,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:42.660
the debate that if market economy

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.840
or the state in the economy,

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.170
we sorted this problem in 2006,

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.350
because we were a point that

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.310
the state has to be in the economy.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.130
It's a very important role of the economy,

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:04.130
has to be related to the
state and not to the market,

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:09.590
because the market, it's not
equilibrium in the market,

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.353
and normally there's no
equilibrium in the market.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.910
And normally, in the
market are two players,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.910
offers and demanders,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.703
but one of them is always winning.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.123
It's not an equilibrium in that market.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.500
So, for us, it's very important
that the state has to be,

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.870
it has to appropriate it,

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:44.870
to get the surplus of the economy,

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:49.020
in order to do the third
basic of the model,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:50.643
which is, redistribute.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.600
The principle role of the state

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.220
has to be to redistribute it,

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:59.100
because we are facing now,
and there are many books,

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:03.060
Mr. Picket is doing a good job there,

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:07.380
trying to understand the
inequality in the world,

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:11.160
but who's going to resolve
the inequality, the market?

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.080
No, from our point of view,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.753
the market never will solve the problem.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:21.060
Who's going to be the
solver of the problem,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.210
is, of course, the state.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.513
So, generate surpluses, exigence,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.250
the state appropriates
it and redistribute.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:33.090
Why?

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.250
We would like to redistribute

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.853
income surpluses in the economy,

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:43.410
because we were to resolve
the inequality and the poverty

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.813
that we were facing in 2005.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.890
So, that's the principles. It's very easy.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:51.240
When

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.660
you look at models, and
Professor Tobin shows us

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:58.980
in a very good manners in many researches,

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.813
but the models have to be very
simple in order to succeed,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.680
and the principles of our
model, as you can see,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.613
it's very easy.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.880
To generate exigence

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.030
from the natural resources in my country,

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:18.870
redistribute it among people by the state,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.780
to resolve the problem of
poverty and inequalities.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.920
That's it.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.417
That's the model in a very
easy way and very short way.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.060
That's the way our model works.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:36.030
On the left hand side, we
have the natural resources.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.700
We have hydrocarbons, mining, electricity,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.830
all the strategic sectors.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.070
We get the money from those sectors

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.890
in order to redistribute them.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.020
The state is in the middle of it.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.990
Receives the money on the surpluses,

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.380
and do the redistribution process.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.280
Not only generating a new country,

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.543
industrialized country,

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.230
with industry, manufacturing, and so on,

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:08.250
generate income, employment, and so on,

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:12.420
but also, as you can see
in the bottom of the graph,

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.700
you see the income redistribution

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.533
and poverty reduction process,

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:20.590
which comes from the income,

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:23.970
the resources that we
generate in the surpluses

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.310
from the natural resource, that's it.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:30.397
It's very simple, the
principles that our model has.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:36.120
And in this one,

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.940
you have some of the differences

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:40.593
with the neo-liberalism model,

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:44.610
which was in place in Bolivia since 1985,

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.403
as you can see it, up to 2005.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.340
In the right hand side of the graph,

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.730
you see the Economic Social
Communitarian Productive Model,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:57.420
which was two phases of it.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:02.420
One from 2006 up to November, 2019,

00:13:04.875 --> 00:13:09.513
which we suffer a coup d'etat in Bolivia.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.533
The neo-liberalism model came again,

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:17.883
wrongly, from my point of view.

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:23.930
And then when we restart
our model in November, 2020,

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:28.620
when I become the president of my country.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:33.240
We restarted, and of course,
we have a good results,

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.390
we will see later on.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:38.973
But those are the principle differences.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.830
The first one is the most debated one,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:47.070
which is the free market
against the active presence

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.530
and participation of the
state in the economy.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:50.553
That's the debate.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:53.730
It's a long story.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:58.730
In economics, it's a
long story about that,

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.360
but in our case,

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.633
in a less developing country,

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:09.560
where we have a many indigenous
people, poverty, inequality,

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:17.370
non-industrialized country, and so on,

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:20.700
for us, is a solution that the state

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.010
can resolve this problem,

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:25.683
and that's what we have done so far.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:32.883
One of interesting things is
that the neo-liberalism model,

00:14:33.983 --> 00:14:36.520
and now every time that we are looking at

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.433
every year is less dependent, but,

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:46.680
at the beginning, what
the neo-liberalism model

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.243
used to say is that,

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:52.563
in order to grow, a country should export.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:55.797
We had a president in Bolivia

00:14:55.797 --> 00:14:59.133
who used to say, export or die.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:04.533
That was the beginning of the
neo-liberalism in my country.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:10.910
And in my country, we
just die trying to export.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:18.110
So, what we have done is to
change the engine of the model.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:22.380
Instead to be the external demand,

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:23.973
should be the internal demand.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:29.670
We should base the growth on people,

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.993
because people need to
get better life every day,

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.586
become better days for everybody,

00:15:37.586 --> 00:15:41.040
and that is consumption that's
investment in a country,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:43.923
not working for other countries.

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:48.240
So, those are principles, I
would say, more important,

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:53.240
in order to resume the
main ideas of the model.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.820
Now,

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:01.080
we turn in some results that
many of them, of course,

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.680
are in the IMF, World Bank report,

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:07.233
and many international institutions.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:09.630
The first one is the growth rate.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:14.060
You can see in the average
growth rate from 1985, 2005,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.410
it means the neo-liberalism model,

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:23.253
the growth rate was 3% in average.

00:16:24.699 --> 00:16:27.510
And in our government, and
it's the first government,

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.243
2006 and 2019,

00:16:31.297 --> 00:16:36.297
4.7%, we increase very
clearly the growth rates.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:42.093
And of course, here 2019,

00:16:43.244 --> 00:16:46.860
and 2020, about the COVID-19 and so on,

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:48.990
the pandemic and so on,

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:50.670
affected, of course, all the countries,

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:54.003
and it is the reason we have
a decreasing for 8.7% growth.

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.200
So,

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:03.010
we were one of the countries
in South America that have

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:07.533
the worst economic
performance at the time.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:13.520
When the neo-liberalism model
came again in the government

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:18.633
who give the coup d'etat in Bolivia.

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:22.350
Now, we are increasing at 4%,

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:26.163
is the growth rate
estimated for this year,

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.180
and I think we can do that better.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.880
We will see it, what we have done.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.320
Another thing is that
many times, for six times,

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:41.940
we have it put it in the screen now,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:46.050
but Bolivia was, in six times,

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:51.050
the first growth economic
rate in the region.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:59.573
Some of them were 2014, 2018.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:04.710
But of course, if you just look at 2020,

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:07.800
when neo-liberalism came again to Bolivia,

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.160
we were almost the last
one in growth rate,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:13.623
but with a negative one.

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:19.910
So, it means that our model
used to have better results

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:25.887
than when we were in the
neo-liberalism, okay.

00:18:25.887 --> 00:18:27.570
And this is very important,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:30.153
we divided the growth rate in Bolivia,

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:35.040
for the source of this growth rate.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.917
If you look at the blue bar,

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.803
it is the internal demand,

00:18:43.803 --> 00:18:47.250
which this graph shows you,

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:49.830
that the internal demand is very important

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:53.850
to give us the growth
rates, which it means,

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.580
internal demand means consumption,

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.553
means investments in my country,

00:19:01.912 --> 00:19:05.010
which are the main issues

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:09.150
that are driving the growth in my country.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.980
Which is very clear with our model,

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.120
the theoretical issue of the model,

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:23.070
the internal demand is
driving the growth rate,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:28.070
not the external demand, which
is the exports minus imports,

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.270
which is the external demand,

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:35.270
which is also negative for
us and many, many times.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:39.660
And in those years where we
benefit from the exports,

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:41.730
of course, it works together,

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:45.510
the external demand,
plus the internal demand

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:49.083
succeed in increasing
the Bolivian economy.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:55.950
This another one shows very
important thing for us,

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.363
because at that time,

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:04.710
everybody look at our
government as a socialist one,

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:09.330
and everybody is scared
about the socialism.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:13.410
And they say, socialists
will take off from you

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:17.710
all your goods and so
on, and there will be no

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:23.130
private enterprises,
because the socialists,

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.640
what they want is to keep
everything for the state

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:30.330
and no private enterprises at all.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:31.863
That's what they used to say,

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:36.870
and this graph shows you
that in our government,

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.830
under our model, the
private sector increase

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:45.830
much more than they even did
in the neo-liberalist model,

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:49.320
which of course, shows you very clearly

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:54.320
that is absolutely compatible
with the private sector,

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:57.150
because it's very easy to understand that.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.450
When the public sector invest,

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:03.060
it is like a train,

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:07.350
that all the cars afterwards

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.203
are pulled by the state.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:15.270
Everybody, when the state invests,

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:18.000
there is a multiplier impact

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:20.520
in other sectors, private sectors,

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:22.293
and that's why you have the impact.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.260
If you look at 2005,

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:30.260
only 64,000 private enterprises
where were in Bolivia,

00:21:33.387 --> 00:21:36.310
and now we have more than 355,000

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:41.400
private enterprises working in my country.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.750
So, it is very clear that
we have an impact also

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.853
in the private sector with our model.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:54.450
The per capita GDP,
which is very important.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:59.450
We started in 2005 with
only 1,000 U.S. dollars

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:01.263
per capita income.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:07.280
Now, we are 3,437 U.S. dollars.

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:09.753
So we multiply by three,

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:14.760
per capita GDP in our country,

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:16.863
which is very important, as you know.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:22.113
The inflation is not a
problem in my country.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:27.000
Even now, accumulate inflation rate

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:32.000
from January to August is
only 1.6% of inflation rate.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:38.120
Even in these years, when we all know

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.890
that the inflation is
a worry for everybody,

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:44.670
especially here in
United States, in Europe,

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:48.810
they worry about what's going
on with the food prices,

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:51.420
with the energy prices and so on,

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:54.810
it has not worry in our country,

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.530
because we have the lowest
inflation in our region,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:00.060
and maybe,

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:03.300
would be the lowest one of the countries

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:08.300
which has had the lower inflation
in the world, I would say.

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.350
So, it is very important because,

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:13.140
we are taking care about poor people.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:17.370
Our model takes care of poor people.

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:18.870
We look for them.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:21.030
I mean, we try to do everything

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:25.620
for protecting all the poor
people who lives in my country.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:30.090
That's why inflation is the
worst thing for poor people,

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.673
because rich people, they
know how to protect them,

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.610
but poor people, they
don't have instruments

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.980
in order to protect
from the inflation rate.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.803
So that's why we take
care of the inflation.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:47.880
The trade balance has
improved in this year.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:52.110
From 2021 to 2022 we
have positive numbers.

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:55.290
In the past we were
looking at negative numbers

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.390
in the trade balance,

00:23:57.390 --> 00:24:00.990
but now we are improving
because we have a policy

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.740
which substitute imports,
import substitution, we call it.

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:07.950
The import substitution process,

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:11.070
we put it in place in November, 2020,

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:13.650
and has giving us good results.

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:15.300
We are improving exports,

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:17.880
but also we are reducing imports,

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.460
showing that the Bolivia has a big,

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:22.053
and enormous, I would say,

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:27.540
production potential that we
are using now in order to reach

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:30.483
these figures, as you
can see in the graph.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:35.640
Savings and a number of
deposits is very important.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:40.640
Look at the flat savings
in the financial system,

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.503
in the neo-liberalism model,

00:24:44.357 --> 00:24:49.357
you have in the graph here,
in the left hand side graph.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:52.413
In 1997, up to 2005,

00:24:55.408 --> 00:24:56.760
in the neo-liberalism model,

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:00.510
almost flat, the savings of people,

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:05.343
and the saving started to
increase with our economic model.

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:11.450
And we have from 5,000
billion U.S. dollar,

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.910
we have now 31,000 billion.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.940
So, we have a lot of
increase in the saving.

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:20.553
People have more money,
that's what they say.

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:24.390
And also in the right hand side graph,

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:29.343
you see the increase of the loans.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:37.380
Sorry, the number of accounts.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:40.380
It's very important, this number of...

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:43.333
We started in 2005 with 1,900

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:50.763
of accounts in my country,

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:55.633
and now we have more
than 14,000 of accounts.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:02.880
It means that more people now have more

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.550
deposits at the banks,

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:07.590
which is very important
in developing countries,

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.590
because it means that not
only they have money now,

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:13.060
but the income they have

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:18.270
is more than the expenditures they have,

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:21.570
so they have a margin of saving.

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:25.740
And those savings now are
increasing in the deposits,

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.440
and in the number of accounts as well,

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:29.490
which is very important.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:31.653
And when we divide it,

00:26:33.092 --> 00:26:35.220
we would like to see if

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:38.940
the number of accounts and the money

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:41.280
that is getting in the bank

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:44.160
belongs to rich people or poor people.

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:46.320
We look at, when we divide it,

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:48.660
the right hand side graphic,

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:53.100
the people that has less than $500

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:57.627
deposited in the banks, and so on,

00:26:57.627 --> 00:27:02.627
had more than 5,000 U.S.
dollars deposited at the banks.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:08.270
And you will see that
87% actually in Bolivia

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.890
of the accounts belong to people

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:14.460
who have only 500 U.S. dollars

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:17.940
or less deposited at the banks.

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:21.480
So, it means that we have democratized

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:24.900
also the economy, the money,
now they have accounts,

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:29.290
and more people they have
accounts now at the banks

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:35.810
are people that have less
resources, less income.

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:41.130
That is very important, because
it shows some of the results

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:44.403
of the redistribution
model that we put in place.

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:48.240
The loans, the financial system loans,

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:51.000
also increase, as you can see.

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:54.423
And the non-performing
loans are very, very low,

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:57.360
comparing with what had happened

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.913
in the neo-liberalism period.

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:04.410
So, if you look at 1997, 2008,

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:09.410
even we have 16% of non-performing loans,

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:14.280
but now we have only 2%
of non-performing loans,

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:18.750
even in a period where
we faced the pandemic,

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:23.750
and everybody used the savings
at the banks in my country,

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:27.300
and also they didn't have money,

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:31.170
and so they didn't pay the
debts, the loans at the banks,

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.420
because they didn't
have money at that time.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.820
So, even though,

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:40.820
we have only 2.2% of
non-performing loans in my country.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:47.073
Also, this is very important,
the Bolivianization process.

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:51.873
In the neo-liberalism model,

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:55.410
Professor Jeffrey Sachs,

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:59.570
who was the advisor of that government

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:02.130
at the beginning of the
neo-liberalism model,

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:06.727
put in place a dollarization
process, in order to face

00:29:08.411 --> 00:29:13.411
the hyperinflation we
suffer in 1982 to 1984,

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:21.177
where we look at an
inflation rate of 21,000%,

00:29:23.692 --> 00:29:27.750
21,000% of inflation, of
annual inflation rate.

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:33.920
So, when we became government in 2006,

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:37.110
we started to do many policies,

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:39.537
in order to digitalize the economy,

00:29:39.537 --> 00:29:42.033
and we succeeded, as you
can see in the graph.

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:44.940
In the past, in the neo-liberalism model,

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:47.430
only 5% in the financial system

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:52.140
were deposits in our domestic currency

00:29:52.140 --> 00:29:53.637
which is in Boliviano.

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:58.320
But now we have 85% of people

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:02.163
is depositing money in domestic currency.

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:07.560
And also in the loans, in
the neo-liberalism model,

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:12.560
7% of the loans were made
in domestic currency,

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:19.220
and now, 99% of the loans
are made in our currency,

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:21.870
demonstrated the confidence
of people in our currency

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:24.660
and our policy, and in all the results

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:28.077
we were getting from our model.

00:30:28.077 --> 00:30:30.360
And now, we don't have problems

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:33.480
with dollarization in the economy.

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:35.950
We are doing very well, and people

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:39.510
trust in our domestic currency,

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:43.410
which is for monetary
policy, very important.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:44.880
If people don't trust in your current,

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:48.330
you cannot do any
monetary policies at all.

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:52.050
So, it is very useful for us to recover

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:55.050
the monetary policy from
the dollarization process,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.450
that the neo-liberalism did in the past.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.250
Of course, Professor Sachs now

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:02.673
is not a neo-liberalist professor,

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:07.200
has changed his mind,
fortunately, I would say,

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.970
but, at the time,

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:11.883
he advised this kind of
dollarization process.

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:17.430
The global and current
fiscal balance gets better.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:22.110
If you look in the neo-liberalism
process, all negative.

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:27.110
We have more or less
nine years of surplus,

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:31.710
and then we started to have,
again, a fiscal deficit.

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:36.480
We were recovering from
that, and then, again,

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:39.180
the coup d'etat came in my country,

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:44.070
and started to do bad,
wrongly economic policies.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:49.070
Again, we have almost 13% of
the GDP as fiscal deficit.

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:52.260
It's enormous one.

00:31:53.209 --> 00:31:54.480
And we started to reduce it.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:59.400
Now, we are looking to have
eight and 7%, and so on.

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:01.260
All these years, we are going to recover

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:04.110
those figures in this fiscal balance,

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.720
but, you know, you can see it easily,

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:08.703
what happened in the fiscal sector.

00:32:10.500 --> 00:32:12.600
It is very important here.

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:15.073
Since we have put in place the

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:21.990
economic social communitarian
productive model

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:23.853
in my country, which is our model.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:30.350
We changed the source of funding
of our public investment.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:35.730
In the past, the public
investment came from

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.800
mainly external resources,

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:39.783
debt, external debt, basically.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:45.950
Now, two thirds of the
investment, the public investment

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.793
in my country come from
the internal resources.

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:55.720
And you can

00:32:56.970 --> 00:32:58.440
ask yourself, what's going on?

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:03.440
Why we changed the source of
the funding of the investment,

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:07.560
the public investment in my country.

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:10.560
And the reason is that when you recover

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:13.293
your natural resources, you have money.

00:33:15.182 --> 00:33:18.660
That money that in the
past were going out away.

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:23.610
We keep the money in my country,

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:25.920
and started to invest the money

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:27.780
that came from the natural resources.

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.720
That's why, in a huge change

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:32.820
in the percentage of funding

00:33:32.820 --> 00:33:37.173
or the source of the funding
of the public investment.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:40.380
Public investment, by the way,

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:43.233
is one of the key issues in our model,

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.423
it comes from the neo-liberalist model,

00:33:49.423 --> 00:33:51.443
where the base of the
neo-liberalism model is, of course,

00:33:52.921 --> 00:33:54.838
the private investment.

00:33:56.290 --> 00:34:00.660
In our model, the state
is the main actor there,

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:04.560
so, the public investment
is the main issue for us,

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:06.930
that's why it changes,

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:09.227
we invest more as a public sector,

00:34:09.227 --> 00:34:11.880
and we have results as you see.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:14.520
And that's why, because we recover

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:17.460
all the natural resources, and so on,

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:22.460
we recover the source of
funding our investments.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:28.410
The medium and long term public debt

00:34:28.410 --> 00:34:33.410
is not a problem in my
country since our model.

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:35.070
But in the past, as you can see,

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:40.070
you have the ratio,
external debt against GDP.

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:46.083
You can see that in the past,

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:54.560
there were many years that
this ratio were more than 50%,

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:59.820
but since our government, since our model,

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:02.040
we changed it, we reduced it,

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:03.040
because we increased

00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:05.943
the denominator of the ratio,

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:08.320
and reduced

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:11.850
the ratio,

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:16.500
and now we have only 28% of the ratio

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:21.500
between external debt against GDP.

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.840
So, we were more comfortable now,

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:28.173
with the figures, macroeconomic
figures, as you can see.

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:32.730
We increased the national minimum wage.

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:36.750
In Bolivia, we have a
minimum wage for everybody.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:41.070
Nobody has to earn a salary,

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:41.913
a wage,

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.580
less than that.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:47.973
Yeah, and here, I think we
have in U.S. dollars, yes.

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:53.340
If you look at 2005, 54 U.S. dollars,

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:56.100
it was the minimum salary in my country.

00:35:56.100 --> 00:36:01.100
And now we have more
than 323 U.S. dollars.

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:05.490
So, in this time, we have increased a lot,

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:07.800
the minimum wages in my country,

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:10.350
so people is getting more money.

00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:15.350
And if you look at the figure,

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:18.270
and comparing the figure
between our country, Bolivia,

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:20.485
in 2005,

00:36:20.485 --> 00:36:25.485
we used to have the lowest minimum wage

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:29.640
in all South America,

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:32.580
and now we are just in the middle.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:37.580
And if we continue with this model,

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:42.153
I think we will getting
better year by year.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:49.320
The unemployment rate is
also a success for us.

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:51.053
We used to have 8%,

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:55.263
almost 9% of unemployment.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:37:00.480
In our government we applied our model,

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:04.883
we have more or less 4%, 4.4%, 4.2%.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:08.930
And, now we have 5.2,

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:14.990
but in 2020, we had 12%
of unemployment rate

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:20.070
under the pandemic.

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:23.700
It was very, very worry about that.

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:25.143
But in any case,

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.350
unemployment is also a variable

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:31.620
that shows you that we
have improved a lot.

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:36.180
And, still, there are some
job to do in this variable,

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:40.953
but, I think, we will succeed
also in reducing unemployment.

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:45.183
Also, when we look at the social results,

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:50.010
we have here, how we
reduce extreme poverty,

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:52.590
and how we compare it with all

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:56.190
the South American countries
did in the same period.

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:58.320
In the left hand side graph,

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:02.160
you will see that we
receive the country with 38%

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:05.310
of extreme poverty in my country,

00:38:05.310 --> 00:38:09.543
and we reduced it up to 11% last year.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:13.740
Even last year we reduce it much more

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:15.300
than we did in the past.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.183
So it means that the
model is working again.

00:38:20.432 --> 00:38:22.937
And when we compare our results

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:27.180
with all the other
countries in South America,

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:29.010
that, of course, all the countries

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:32.100
are trying to reduce the poverty as well,

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:35.530
you compare it, Bolivia reduce

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:39.690
more than everybody in our region.

00:38:39.690 --> 00:38:41.933
We reduced in 27%.

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:47.280
Peru, for example,
reduced it in almost 12%,

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:52.050
in the same period
between 2005 up to 2021.

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:56.010
So it means that our
model not only succeeds

00:38:56.010 --> 00:39:00.120
in getting good macroeconomic results,

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:03.420
but also has good social results,

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:06.450
which is an advantage of our model,

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.750
that the neo-liberalism
model cannot appropriate,

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:13.563
cannot talk about, because
they never reached both.

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:21.020
Also, the income inequality
by Gini Index, we reduced it.

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:25.650
We were a very inequality
country together with Brazil.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:30.650
We were the worst in the region, in 2005.

00:39:31.170 --> 00:39:35.310
We had point 0.60 of Gini Index,

00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:36.663
and now we are only 0.42,

00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:41.790
which is a huge reduction.

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:46.050
And, again, when we compare our results

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:50.523
from those that the countries
in South America get,

00:39:52.129 --> 00:39:54.453
and, again, Bolivia reduced it in 0.15,

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:57.690
and all the other countries

00:39:57.690 --> 00:40:02.610
reduced it less than we
have done with our model.

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:05.070
Taking into account that
many countries still

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:07.653
they are using the neo-liberalism model.

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:14.460
This is another very
important things there.

00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:17.580
The structure of the social stratum,

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:22.580
the composition of people,
according to the income.

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:29.270
In 2005, 60% of people in Bolivia

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:32.580
were in low income conditions,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.280
35% in middle income conditions,

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:38.373
and only 4% in high income conditions.

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:44.570
Now, when you compare, we have
36% in low income conditions.

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:50.463
We reduced it from 60 to 36,

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:54.240
and we have increased
the middle income people

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:57.690
from 35% to 60%,

00:40:57.690 --> 00:41:02.373
almost we doubled the figure from 2005.

00:41:03.570 --> 00:41:07.620
So we have reduced poverty,
we have increased the income,

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:11.550
especially middle income, and
this is not only our figures,

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:15.513
I will show you some
Word Bank figures later.

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:19.080
Also, we reduced the differences

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:23.160
between the richest 10%
people in my country,

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:28.160
against the 10% poorest
people in my country.

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:33.360
When you compare the richest 10% against

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:36.990
the poorest 10%,

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:41.990
we have a difference of
128 times in the past,

00:41:42.570 --> 00:41:46.466
but now we have only 20 times.

00:41:46.466 --> 00:41:49.080
There's still some job to do.

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:50.790
Yeah, we completely agree,

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:53.100
but we have reduced a lot,

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:55.470
especially in the rural area,

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:57.420
people who live in the rural area.

00:41:57.420 --> 00:42:00.450
They reduce more, the inequality

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:03.033
among rich people and poor people.

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:06.873
And as I promised, here is the World Bank.

00:42:06.873 --> 00:42:10.083
This is a picture of the World Bank.

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:14.100
They issue a report, where it says,

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:17.488
the income growth of the poorest 40%

00:42:17.488 --> 00:42:19.650
of the population, who do the best

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:21.243
in order to reduce,

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:29.480
improves for the income of
the poorest 40% of a country.

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:31.983
And you see here.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:36.123
Bolivia, we have market at the top,

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:42.110
is the country that increase
the income of the poorest 40%

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:48.783
better than any other
country in the world.

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:54.060
It is a World Bank picture,
it's not our picture,

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:55.360
it's a World Bank picture.

00:42:56.400 --> 00:42:59.190
We, according to the World Bank,

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:04.190
we increased the income
growth of the poorest 40%

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:08.890
in 12.5%.

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:15.150
Peru did, let's say, 9%,

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:18.630
nine and something percent, and so on.

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:21.630
So, Bolivia, according to the World Bank,

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:25.470
is doing better in
improving the income growth

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:30.420
of the 40% of poorest
people in my country,

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:32.850
comparing with all of the
countries in the world.

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:35.280
So, those are our results.

00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:37.210
And the last picture I have for you

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:42.660
is this one that is very important for us,

00:43:42.660 --> 00:43:45.461
which is the expectancy,

00:43:45.461 --> 00:43:48.663
the life expectancy at birth.

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:54.420
Bolivia, if you look at the,
let's say, the blue bars,

00:43:55.830 --> 00:44:00.830
we have a 64 years old of
life expectancy in 2005,

00:44:03.990 --> 00:44:07.053
now we have increased to 74 years.

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:11.490
And you have all the countries
there in South America,

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:16.233
but in the picture that is in the bottom,

00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:18.363
you will see,

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:25.310
comparing all the increases
in life expectancy

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:29.820
in the region, Bolivia has increased

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:33.780
almost 11 years of life expectancy,

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:38.780
which is the highest
increase in life expectancy

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:40.860
in the region.

00:44:40.860 --> 00:44:43.950
For example, Brazil
increased in five years old

00:44:43.950 --> 00:44:45.450
in the same period,

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:49.260
but Bolivia did almost 11 years.

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:53.823
So that's why, I'll finish
it with this figure,

00:44:55.500 --> 00:44:59.190
we say that our model has succeeded,

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:01.470
not only in the economic side,

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:05.190
but also, more important
for us, in the social side,

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:08.613
because if we have a more equal country,

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:14.600
we have a better country,
a better feeded country,

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:18.513
a better healthy country,

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:23.613
we will have more and better
results and a better society.

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:26.840
That's the objective, when I say

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:29.040
at the beginning of the model.

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:31.800
Reduce the poverty, well, we have done,

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:35.100
and reduce inequalities,
that also we have done,

00:45:35.100 --> 00:45:36.810
we have reached with this model.

00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:37.995
Thank you very much.

00:45:37.995 --> 00:45:41.162
(audience applauding)

00:45:47.889 --> 00:45:49.830
- Thank you very much, Mr. President.

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:52.980
I think that's a very
enlightening conversation

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:57.060
and hypothesis about
the Bolivian, basically,

00:45:57.060 --> 00:46:00.180
I will say, new paradigm, in
terms of think about growth,

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:02.940
and, I have a set of questions

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:05.790
that I'm gonna actually gonna
start asking the president,

00:46:05.790 --> 00:46:08.100
but I do want this to be a conversation.

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:10.140
So in the sense, if people have questions,

00:46:10.140 --> 00:46:13.050
please, raise your hand, and
I'm gonna give you the chance

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:15.300
to basically ask a question.

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:17.943
But let me start basically first, and,

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:21.210
so, it's fascinating,

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:24.450
and clearly this is, the
data speaks by itself, right?

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:29.040
Thinking about what the new,
let's say economic model,

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:31.200
basically, that you have proposed,

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:32.490
because you were a minister finance

00:46:32.490 --> 00:46:33.420
during this period, right?

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:35.073
So, with Evo Morales.

00:46:36.988 --> 00:46:40.830
If you see the macro numbers
are quite impressive.

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:45.780
Lower inequality, lower
employment, lower inflation,

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:48.780
and at the same time, more resources

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:50.100
collected from the government.

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:52.380
These resources coming
from, as you were saying,

00:46:52.380 --> 00:46:55.470
the resource based aspect of the economy.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:57.170
So I guess I have questions about,

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:00.180
thinking about the long run.

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:03.720
So, how do you think about first lessons?

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:05.040
What are the micro lessons?

00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:06.720
I'm gonna be asking that in a second,

00:47:06.720 --> 00:47:07.950
but more thinking about the long run.

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:11.220
How do we, a country like Bolivia,

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:14.940
that really depends on
the prices of commodities,

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:16.380
so, currently you were saying about

00:47:16.380 --> 00:47:18.210
the importance of the internal market,

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:20.010
but these resources are global.

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:21.240
When you're thinking about lithium,

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:23.910
when you're thinking about
other commodities in the world,

00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:26.340
they're driven by global
supply and demand.

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:28.590
So how do we make this more
sustainable in the long run?

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:29.640
So, I'll give you an example.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:33.240
Chile, which has been an exporter
of copper for many years,

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:35.970
they have basically adapted a fiscal rule.

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:39.150
A fiscal rule to basically
guarantee, somehow,

00:47:39.150 --> 00:47:40.740
like a level of expanding,

00:47:40.740 --> 00:47:44.580
that it's not fluctuating
with the commodity prices.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:48.450
Another example is Uruguay.
No surprise I'm from Uruguay.

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:50.370
They also just adapted a fiscal rule.

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:53.220
We don't have a resource based economy

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:55.470
as the case of Bolivia,
and there's other resources

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:57.480
that Uruguay basically produces,

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:00.030
but we do get fluctuations
and commodity prices,

00:48:00.030 --> 00:48:02.820
and that can explain a big component

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.140
of these macro imbalances.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:05.789
So, I guess moving forward,

00:48:05.789 --> 00:48:07.830
and I apologize for the question,

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:08.899
it's more a challenging
question, which is--

00:48:08.899 --> 00:48:10.200
- A very good question, I would say.

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:11.033
- Sorry?

00:48:11.033 --> 00:48:12.450
- I would say it's a very good question.

00:48:12.450 --> 00:48:14.310
- Thank you. Thank you very much for that.

00:48:14.310 --> 00:48:16.260
So, yeah, sorry. So, what do you think?

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:18.420
How do we generate something that's

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:20.880
sustainable more in the
long run, let's say?

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:23.463
- Rules not always work.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:31.690
And what happened with
rules, and those kind of

00:48:33.780 --> 00:48:36.150
ways to my eye, it's the economy

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.133
usually break down very quickly.

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:42.240
Because they are not prepared for

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:44.400
crisis like this one, for example,

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:46.800
and they broke the rules.

00:48:46.800 --> 00:48:49.749
And those rules, for example,

00:48:49.749 --> 00:48:54.120
European Union, it was
3% of the fiscal deficit.

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:55.000
I don't know

00:48:56.190 --> 00:49:00.000
how they will work, I mean...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.000
But, anyway, the better rule
is the rule that is flexible

00:49:06.300 --> 00:49:07.353
in my point of view.

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:10.403
And,

00:49:11.491 --> 00:49:12.990
in order to answer your question,

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:15.190
which is a very good
question, the long run.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:21.660
In the long run, we have to industrialize

00:49:21.660 --> 00:49:24.180
our natural resources,

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:27.510
we have to increase
the production of food,

00:49:27.510 --> 00:49:30.270
the agriculture sectors,
we have to improve it,

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:35.223
in order to have no
import dependence at all.

00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:38.640
And that's what we're working on.

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:42.060
If you look at what
happened in this crisis,

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:46.743
for example, fertilizer,
agrochemicals, you know, and so on,

00:49:48.420 --> 00:49:53.420
went up, and everybody cried,
and food raised prices,

00:49:53.640 --> 00:49:56.520
and there were not food
all over the world.

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:58.833
Everybody was looking where to buy food.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:04.710
What we have to do is improve
the agriculture sectors,

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:07.020
to improve productivity,

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:10.230
to become less dependent

00:50:10.230 --> 00:50:13.440
at least, if not totally independent

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:18.440
from the imports of
inputs, seeds, and so on,

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:23.160
because everybody is looking
for seeds for everybody,

00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:26.760
and that's the problem,
that is the problem.

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:30.692
In my country, we started
to face this problem,

00:50:30.692 --> 00:50:35.692
to do some policies in order
to avoid this problem in 2020.

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:41.370
When I became president of my country,

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:44.190
we started to issue policies,

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:48.150
we started the import
substitution process,

00:50:48.150 --> 00:50:51.480
policies we put in place, and so on.

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:54.681
That is the difference
we have these results,

00:50:54.681 --> 00:50:59.040
all the people that was
trusting on the globalization,

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:02.340
trusting on imports, and free market,

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.670
and those things that never worked.

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:09.000
Because when there is
crisis, nobody's your friend.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:10.953
Everybody's looking themselves,

00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:14.460
and that's why we are facing this problem.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:16.593
So, agriculture.

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:23.850
If you don't have last
model cars, it doesn't care,

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:28.290
but if does care if you don't have food.

00:51:28.290 --> 00:51:31.203
So that's the first thing
that the world has to do it.

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:34.803
In our case,

00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:39.270
countries which have natural resource,

00:51:39.270 --> 00:51:41.733
they have to naturalize
it as soon as possible.

00:51:42.930 --> 00:51:45.480
And, of course, in our point of view,

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:49.053
according to the respecting
the mother earth,

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:55.110
ecologic development,
which is very important.

00:51:55.110 --> 00:51:59.400
It's most costly, but we have to do it.

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:01.923
Rich countries didn't
do it at that moment,

00:52:03.750 --> 00:52:04.583
we have to do it.

00:52:04.583 --> 00:52:07.950
So, in our countries it's much costly

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:12.416
to become a developed
country, but we have to do it.

00:52:12.416 --> 00:52:15.390
We have to develop all
the natural resources,

00:52:15.390 --> 00:52:19.023
respecting, I say again,
with mother earth.

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:23.760
Then you have to invest in education,

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:25.980
you have to invest in health,

00:52:25.980 --> 00:52:27.747
you have to invest in many things,

00:52:27.747 --> 00:52:29.283
and that is the long run.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:31.190
That is the long run.

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:34.320
If you have food, and you have education,

00:52:34.320 --> 00:52:36.600
you have investment technology,

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:40.380
you have a long term growth rate.

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:41.400
- This is great. This is great.

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:43.827
He's talking about sources of growth,

00:52:43.827 --> 00:52:46.890
and this goes back to the
textbook, "Economics Models".

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:49.890
So, human capital seems to be important,

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:53.040
of course, investment,
private and public investment.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:55.980
And I guess what we're learning
from Bolivia is more like,

00:52:55.980 --> 00:52:57.810
so, we know the ingredients, the recipe,

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:59.790
it's a very local recipe,

00:52:59.790 --> 00:53:01.893
the Bolivian way of doing this.

00:53:02.790 --> 00:53:05.760
Anyone has a particular
question, would like to ask?

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:08.520
Yes, please. Yes.

00:53:08.520 --> 00:53:10.680
- [Man] Thanks, Mr.
President, I'll stand up.

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:12.987
My name is Tobias Sathe, I'm from Ecuador.

00:53:12.987 --> 00:53:16.170
I'm a master student here
at the Yale Jackson School.

00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:17.340
Thank you again for being here.

00:53:17.340 --> 00:53:19.770
Congratulations on your economic policies,

00:53:19.770 --> 00:53:22.740
the stability and growth that
your country has experienced,

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:25.260
especially in this post pandemic period.

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:27.180
And especially for your
focused and commitment

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:29.910
of improving the lives of
those that are most in need

00:53:29.910 --> 00:53:32.610
of the poorest household in your country.

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:36.270
My question relates to a
various contrast perhaps,

00:53:36.270 --> 00:53:38.130
between your foreign policy,

00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:40.200
and your domestic economic model.

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:42.720
So, domestically, as
you've explained today,

00:53:42.720 --> 00:53:45.060
is to prioritize improving

00:53:45.060 --> 00:53:47.260
the living conditions
of those most in need.

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:50.610
However, in your foreign policy,

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:52.950
you do recognize and support

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:55.740
the regime of Nicolas Maduro in Venezuela.

00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:58.710
Is this not to you an
apparent contradiction

00:53:58.710 --> 00:54:01.710
to support a regime that
has been responsible

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:03.177
for the largest amount of human suffering

00:54:03.177 --> 00:54:05.683
in our nearby history in the region,

00:54:05.683 --> 00:54:09.120
a country that now 76% of Venezuelans

00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:13.050
live in extreme poverty
under less than $1.90 a day.

00:54:13.050 --> 00:54:15.600
So how can you gain to
support, in general,

00:54:15.600 --> 00:54:16.680
improving the lives of the poor

00:54:16.680 --> 00:54:19.770
while also supporting the regime of

00:54:19.770 --> 00:54:21.510
Nicolas Maduro in Venezuela?

00:54:21.510 --> 00:54:22.890
Thank you.

00:54:22.890 --> 00:54:27.363
- Well, actually we don't
support any government except us.

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:29.153
Alright?

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:33.810
What we do have is good
relations with everybody,

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:35.253
even with United States.

00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:42.800
We don't have bad relations
with European Union.

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:46.450
European Union and many countries

00:54:47.520 --> 00:54:51.303
participate actively in
the coup d'etat in 2019,

00:54:52.800 --> 00:54:55.113
but we still have relations.

00:54:57.630 --> 00:55:00.420
Bolivia is a country
who always will maintain

00:55:00.420 --> 00:55:02.130
good relations with all the countries,

00:55:02.130 --> 00:55:03.993
that is our foreign policy.

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:08.670
We have good relations
with Chile, for example,

00:55:08.670 --> 00:55:11.853
we have a huge problem there about sea.

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:13.980
Alright?

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:16.260
But Bolivia is always not supporting,

00:55:16.260 --> 00:55:17.860
we don't support any government.

00:55:19.170 --> 00:55:21.873
We have good relations
with the governments.

00:55:22.710 --> 00:55:23.820
So, it's a difference.

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:28.470
We respect the auto
determination of the nations.

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:30.150
If they want to do what they do,

00:55:30.150 --> 00:55:33.480
they want to do in their country,

00:55:33.480 --> 00:55:35.883
it's their problem, not our problem.

00:55:36.840 --> 00:55:40.830
We have to respect the
way they decide to live,

00:55:40.830 --> 00:55:44.310
decide to do elections, or not elections,

00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:46.710
or whatever regime, political regime,

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:48.513
they decide to adopt,

00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:52.080
the economic policy they decide to do.

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:54.210
We live in a country where
there are many countries

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:57.570
that still they're applying,
and their university

00:55:57.570 --> 00:56:01.710
are still teaching the
neo-liberalism model,

00:56:01.710 --> 00:56:03.660
and we respect that.

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:04.743
I mean, it's a way.

00:56:05.700 --> 00:56:08.703
It doesn't means that we support them.

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:13.320
I think we have to live in a world

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:15.603
where we have to respect each other.

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:18.660
That's the best way, I would say,

00:56:18.660 --> 00:56:22.293
to live in peace with everybody.

00:56:23.220 --> 00:56:25.080
- Thank you, Mr. President.

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:27.960
Let me ask a question, going
back to your presentation.

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:29.520
And we can ask someone else basically,

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:30.810
who wants to ask a question.

00:56:30.810 --> 00:56:34.500
So, I guess, something that
was quite surprising to me

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:38.730
was in the model that has been working,

00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:40.290
let's say, in Bolivia,

00:56:40.290 --> 00:56:41.880
this idea of taking resources

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:43.710
from what used to be
from the private sector

00:56:43.710 --> 00:56:45.210
now belongs to the government.

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:46.740
The idea of the resources.

00:56:46.740 --> 00:56:49.650
Where do we get this resource
bases on natural resources?

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:50.483
So the question is,

00:56:50.483 --> 00:56:53.240
but at the same time you were
showing a lot of firm entry,

00:56:54.780 --> 00:56:57.150
firms have to be actually
going up over time.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:56:59.370
So I guess, one would actually,

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:01.650
as an economist,
basically, maybe, will say,

00:57:01.650 --> 00:57:04.980
in priori, one would expect
something on the country.

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:06.150
If it is the case that the government

00:57:06.150 --> 00:57:07.890
is collecting rent from firms,

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:10.500
that will dis incentivize firm entry

00:57:10.500 --> 00:57:12.510
and firms investing in the country.

00:57:12.510 --> 00:57:14.678
So, how did this happen?

00:57:14.678 --> 00:57:16.950
So, I'm thinking about learning.

00:57:16.950 --> 00:57:18.510
Is it in different sectors?

00:57:18.510 --> 00:57:21.240
How come we are still
generating incentives

00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:23.730
for firms to invest and enter in Bolivia,

00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:25.530
when at the same time you're telling us

00:57:25.530 --> 00:57:27.090
that some of these resources actually

00:57:27.090 --> 00:57:30.090
are now collected to go to
the government and the people?

00:57:30.090 --> 00:57:32.100
- Okay. Well, we--

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:34.223
- We're looking for lessons.
- We differentiate.

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:38.293
If there is international enterprise

00:57:38.293 --> 00:57:41.613
that like to come to Bolivia to work,

00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:46.470
and own the natural resources.

00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:48.000
- Like the land?

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:49.560
- For example,

00:57:49.560 --> 00:57:51.333
or mining, or hydrocarbons.

00:57:53.550 --> 00:57:58.550
The constitution says that at least 51%

00:57:58.620 --> 00:58:00.213
has to belong to the state.

00:58:01.980 --> 00:58:04.860
So we can make some business,

00:58:04.860 --> 00:58:07.110
but in natural resources

00:58:07.110 --> 00:58:10.683
more than 51% has to be for the state.

00:58:12.150 --> 00:58:13.800
That's one of the restrictions.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:18.393
In other sectors, tourism,
services, industry, whatever,

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:20.910
you don't have any limitation.

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:24.993
You can go to 100%,
whatever, no problem at all.

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:27.450
But for the natural resources,

00:58:27.450 --> 00:58:32.450
we have a restriction very
clearly in the constitution.

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:33.930
- Very interesting.

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:36.993
Anyone else would like to... Yes, please.

00:58:36.993 --> 00:58:38.435
- [Woman] I have a question, and if I may,

00:58:38.435 --> 00:58:40.707
I will say in Spanish and English or--

00:58:40.707 --> 00:58:43.485
- Yes. I mean, I was
doing in English, but...

00:58:43.485 --> 00:58:47.568
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:51.480
- [Woman] My question,

00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:54.540
I really appreciated you
mentioning mother earth.

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:57.240
I think it's a very important concept

00:58:57.240 --> 00:58:59.523
that is not discussed enough,

00:59:00.628 --> 00:59:05.628
and being Bolivia, such a rich
country in natural resources,

00:59:06.180 --> 00:59:11.180
what has been the main challenge
to respect mother earth?

00:59:13.902 --> 00:59:17.985
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:59:36.065 --> 00:59:38.342
- I answer in Spanish or in English?

00:59:38.342 --> 00:59:39.175
(all laughing)

00:59:39.175 --> 00:59:40.380
- Let's start in English

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:42.876
and then we can maybe switch to Spanish.

00:59:42.876 --> 00:59:44.880
- (chuckles) Spanish or English.

00:59:44.880 --> 00:59:45.824
- Oh. You choose.

00:59:45.824 --> 00:59:47.580
(President Arce laughs)

00:59:47.580 --> 00:59:49.800
I think some people wanted to
hear in English also, but--

00:59:49.800 --> 00:59:51.630
- Okay. Okay.

00:59:51.630 --> 00:59:55.803
Well, of course, for
every developing country,

00:59:57.060 --> 01:00:00.510
the worst problem that we're facing

01:00:00.510 --> 01:00:02.733
is the cost of technology.

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:07.563
We would like, for example,
to produce clean energy,

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:11.223
but this clean energy, it is very costly.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:17.330
That's why you always, in
the international forums,

01:00:17.370 --> 01:00:20.940
we say that developed
countries should sell us,

01:00:20.940 --> 01:00:25.940
we don't want that they
give us that as a gift,

01:00:26.070 --> 01:00:31.070
but, they should sell us at
very low prices the technology,

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:36.570
in order to develop our countries,
respecting mother earth.

01:00:36.570 --> 01:00:38.640
Because it's very costly to...

01:00:38.640 --> 01:00:40.653
For example, roads,

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:45.900
you should, taking into account the cost,

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:48.993
you should build a straight road,

01:00:50.250 --> 01:00:55.250
but in the middle you have
a natural reserve, whatever,

01:00:56.040 --> 01:00:59.583
you have to go around
it, it's most costly.

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:05.670
And who will give us the
money for that? Nobody.

01:01:05.670 --> 01:01:07.230
And everybody says in the world,

01:01:07.230 --> 01:01:09.543
everybody has to respect mother earth,

01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:11.823
but it's costly.

01:01:13.542 --> 01:01:15.570
And it's much costly for us

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:19.020
to develop in this kind of circumstances.

01:01:19.020 --> 01:01:22.140
But, I think this is the main issue,

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:25.290
the cost of the technology,
the cost of developing,

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:28.650
that the developed countries
should take into account,

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:32.700
in order to make policies,
in order to respect,

01:01:32.700 --> 01:01:37.700
to avoid some crisis that could be

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:41.403
in the ecological surrounding, and so on.

01:01:43.350 --> 01:01:45.630
- Very interesting. No, no, no.

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:47.610
I Wanna go... Yes.

01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:49.193
I don't know which one. Let's do it.

01:01:53.456 --> 01:01:56.970
(speaking in a foreign language)

01:01:56.970 --> 01:01:58.907
Good morning, Mr. President.

01:01:58.907 --> 01:02:02.067
(all laughing)

01:02:02.067 --> 01:02:06.150
(speaking in a foreign language)

01:05:06.652 --> 01:05:08.910
Super interesting.

01:05:08.910 --> 01:05:10.029
Clearly very important question,

01:05:10.029 --> 01:05:11.790
and definitely the answer.

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:16.110
I guess we have time maybe
for one more question.

01:05:16.110 --> 01:05:17.100
That's okay, or?

01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:19.680
- One more question.
- One is okay.

01:05:19.680 --> 01:05:20.513
So,

01:05:21.360 --> 01:05:22.764
yes, I gonna give the... Sorry.

01:05:22.764 --> 01:05:25.560
She raised her hand before, so, yes.

01:05:25.560 --> 01:05:28.692
- [Student] Yeah. Thank
you so much Mr. President.

01:05:28.692 --> 01:05:31.609
(student speaking)

01:06:12.378 --> 01:06:14.477
- It's a good issue.

01:06:14.477 --> 01:06:16.667
In all the South American countries,

01:06:16.667 --> 01:06:19.983
one of the huge problems is corruption,

01:06:21.870 --> 01:06:25.803
especially in the public institutions.

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:31.323
We can find lot of cases and examples.

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:37.290
That's why we are very, very demanding

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:41.550
with our public enterprises.

01:06:41.550 --> 01:06:44.190
The difference is that the state,

01:06:44.190 --> 01:06:46.167
the different institutions of the state,

01:06:46.167 --> 01:06:48.360
and the public enterprises,

01:06:48.360 --> 01:06:52.620
we have public enterprises
where those enterprises

01:06:52.620 --> 01:06:56.040
manage the natural resources,

01:06:56.040 --> 01:06:58.860
hydrocarbons, mining, and so on.

01:06:58.860 --> 01:07:02.640
So, we have created a ministry

01:07:02.640 --> 01:07:06.060
which is in charge of corruption,

01:07:06.060 --> 01:07:07.893
or fighting against corruption,

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:14.640
which is in charge of
all these institutions

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:19.640
that have to report to these ministry,

01:07:20.310 --> 01:07:25.310
in order to clarify,
transparency, everything,

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:28.923
in the process of licitation,
sellings, buyings,

01:07:30.510 --> 01:07:35.510
and all what the public
enterprises do in their activities.

01:07:36.810 --> 01:07:39.960
So, it is very important, this issue.

01:07:39.960 --> 01:07:42.750
So far, we didn't have huge problems

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:43.890
with the public enterprise,

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:48.890
because as everybody look
at the public enterprises

01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:54.200
as the source of money for
growth, for everybody benefit.

01:07:56.160 --> 01:07:59.730
So everybody is looking
at what is is going on

01:07:59.730 --> 01:08:01.563
with the public enterprises.

01:08:03.270 --> 01:08:08.270
We have a law that two times a
year, the public enterprises,

01:08:09.090 --> 01:08:11.940
all the public enterprises in Bolivia

01:08:11.940 --> 01:08:16.940
have to do a public report

01:08:17.250 --> 01:08:18.603
for what they are doing,

01:08:20.370 --> 01:08:23.433
show the figures of their enterprise,

01:08:25.590 --> 01:08:28.230
they have to, twice a year,

01:08:28.230 --> 01:08:32.580
everybody has access
to all the information

01:08:32.580 --> 01:08:35.370
about the public enterprise.

01:08:35.370 --> 01:08:36.987
That's the way we are facing it.

01:08:36.987 --> 01:08:40.413
And also, a more practical issue,

01:08:41.339 --> 01:08:45.240
when a enterprise or any public entity

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:46.893
wants to buy something,

01:08:49.230 --> 01:08:51.860
we are introducing an electronic...

01:08:55.410 --> 01:08:57.300
- Auction
- Auction.

01:08:57.300 --> 01:08:59.340
An electronic auction.

01:08:59.340 --> 01:09:02.670
So, it has reduced a lot,

01:09:02.670 --> 01:09:06.360
the Ministry of Economy and Public Finance

01:09:06.360 --> 01:09:08.880
report to me two weeks ago,

01:09:08.880 --> 01:09:12.910
that we save money more than 300 million

01:09:14.250 --> 01:09:18.570
using these kind of electronic auctions.

01:09:18.570 --> 01:09:20.520
So, it's more transparency,
it's immediately,

01:09:20.520 --> 01:09:25.083
because everybody, let's say
the state wants to buy paper,

01:09:26.010 --> 01:09:28.780
we put in the Web, we want to buy 100

01:09:32.190 --> 01:09:34.920
blocks of paper, whatever,

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:37.353
and everybody puts their offers there,

01:09:38.250 --> 01:09:43.250
and immediately, you know
who who want this buying.

01:09:44.130 --> 01:09:46.260
Immediately, very transparent.

01:09:46.260 --> 01:09:48.870
So, we save a lot of money,

01:09:48.870 --> 01:09:52.020
and we did a more transparent
process in my country.

01:09:52.020 --> 01:09:55.800
We had introduced it in every buying

01:09:55.800 --> 01:09:58.890
and activities of the public sector.

01:09:58.890 --> 01:10:02.520
So we are improving the
transparency, which is very useful,

01:10:02.520 --> 01:10:05.940
and is in line with your worry.

01:10:05.940 --> 01:10:09.090
I think that's the way
we can face the problem,

01:10:09.090 --> 01:10:09.923
transparency.

01:10:11.010 --> 01:10:12.420
- Mr. President, thank you very much

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:15.120
for your talk, for your time,

01:10:15.120 --> 01:10:16.500
and hope to see you again at the

01:10:16.500 --> 01:10:17.880
Jackson School of Global Affairs.

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:20.551
So, thank you very much to the
audience and the questions.

01:10:20.551 --> 01:10:23.301
(all applauding)

01:10:25.546 --> 01:10:29.313
(speaking in a foreign language)

01:10:29.313 --> 01:10:31.980
(Closing music)

